 "3.20", "vulns": []}, {"name": "importlib-metadata", "version": "8.7.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "iniconfig", "version": "2.3.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "jsonschema", "version": "4.25.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "jsonschema-specifications", "version": "2025.9.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "license-expression", "version": "30.4.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "markdown-it-py", "version": "4.2.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "mcp", "version": "1.29.0", "vulns": [{"id": "CVE-2026-59951", "fix_versions": ["1.30.0", "2.2.0"], "aliases": ["GHSA-84m7-p3x7-pcfv"], "description": "### Summary In affected versions, a Streamable HTTP server in stateful mode (the default) never reclaimed session state:  - sessions did not expire; - a client `DELETE` ended the session but left its entry registered; - a session whose first request was refused stayed registered with a running task.  Each session also kept its parsed `initialize` parameters, and the number of sessions was unlimited. A client that could reach the endpoint could grow server memory without bound using ordinary short requests; only a restart reclaimed it.  ### Am I affected? Yes, if you serve MCP over Streamable HTTP in stateful mode and clients you do not fully trust can reach the endpoint. Stateful mode is the default for:  - `FastMCP` (1.x) - `MCPServer` and `Server.streamable_http_app()` (2.x) - `StreamableHTTPSessionManager`  Affected versions: 1.8.0 through 1.29.1, and 2.0.0 through 2.1.1.  Not affected:  - servers on 1.12.0 or later passing `stateless_http=True` (no sessions are kept) - servers using only the stdio or SSE transports  Authentication limits who can open sessions, not how many a token holder can open.  ### Fix Upgrade to `mcp` 1.30.0 (1.x) or 2.2.0 (2.x) or later. In those versions:  - a session with no request in flight for `session_idle_timeout` seconds (default 1800) is closed and its ID then answers `404`; open streams and in-progress requests keep it alive; - at most `max_sessions` sessions exist at once (default 10000); a request that would open another is answered `503`; - a session is discarded as soon as it ends, whether by `DELETE`, expiry, or a refused opening request.  Both settings are keyword arguments on `FastMCP(...)` (1.x), `MCPServer.run()` / `streamable_http_app()` (2.x) and `StreamableHTTPSessionManager`; `None` disables them.  This bounds retention rather than eliminating it: a live session still holds its `initialize` parameters (up to roughly twice the 4 MiB body limit), and an `initialize` with invalid parameters still opens a session that expires like any other. On a small host, lower `session_idle_timeout`, `max_sessions` and `max_request_body_size` to fit.  If you cannot upgrade yet, pass `stateless_http=True` (1.12.0 or later) where you need no per-session state, or restrict who can reach the endpoint."}]}, {"name": "mdurl", "version": "0.1.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "msgpack", "version": "1.2.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-api", "version": "1.37.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-common", "version": "1.37.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http", "version": "1.37.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-instrumentation", "version": "0.58b0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-instrumentation-requests", "version": "0.58b0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-instrumentation-threading", "version": "0.58b0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-proto", "version": "1.37.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-sdk", "version": "1.37.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-semantic-conventions", "version": "0.58b0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "opentelemetry-util-http", "version": "0.58b0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "packageurl-python", "version": "0.17.6", "vulns": []}, {"name": "packaging", "version": "26.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "peewee", "version": "3.19.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pip", "version": "26.2.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pip-api", "version": "0.0.35", "vulns": []}, 